    "original_title": "Mareykanka oo Qorsheynaya Inuu Kala Noqdo Fiisooyinka Ilaa 200,000 oo Magangelyo-doon ah",
    "slug": "us-weighs-revoking-visas-for-up-to-200-000-asylum-applicants",
    "summary": "The Trump administration is considering canceling the business and tourist visas of up to 200,000 foreign nationals in the US who have applied for asylum. The move targets B1 and B2 visa holders who entered the country legally but later filed for protection.",
    "content": "The Trump administration is exploring a policy that would revoke the business and tourist visas of up to 200,000 foreign nationals currently in the United States who have applied for asylum. According to a report by the Associated Press, the plan primarily affects individuals holding B1 and B2 visas, which are issued for business or leisure travel. These visa holders entered the US legally but subsequently submitted asylum applications, placing them under scrutiny.\n\nThe U.S. State Department has stated that it is coordinating with the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) to identify individuals who entered the country on these visa categories and later filed for asylum. Once identified, their visas could be canceled, a move that could complicate their legal status and potentially accelerate deportation proceedings. This initiative is part of broader efforts by the administration to tighten immigration enforcement and reduce the number of pending asylum claims.\n\nThe policy is expected to have significant implications for Somali nationals in the diaspora, many of whom hold B1 or B2 visas while visiting family, attending business conferences, or seeking medical treatment in the US. A substantial number of Somalis in this situation have applied for asylum due to ongoing instability in their homeland, despite being admitted as temporary visitors. If implemented, this measure could force thousands to either leave the country voluntarily or face removal proceedings, creating uncertainty for families and communities already established in the US.\n\nLegal experts caution that revoking visas without individual hearings may violate due process, as asylum applications are independent of visa status. The administration, however, argues that the measure is necessary to prevent abuse of the immigration system, where individuals enter on non-immigrant visas with the intent to stay permanently. For the Somali diaspora, this adds another layer of complexity to an already challenging immigration landscape, prompting many to seek advice from attorneys and community organizations on how to respond.\n\nThe proposal is still under deliberation, and no final decision has been announced. If enacted, it would mark a significant shift in how asylum seekers are treated, particularly those who entered on temporary visas. For now, affected individuals are advised to monitor updates from U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services and consult legal counsel to understand their options.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/25/mareykanka-oo-qorsheynaya-inuu-kala-noqdo-fiisooyinka-ilaa-200000-oo-magangelyo-doon-ah/",
