    "id": "news-1787585397929-1",
    "title": "Somalia's Cabinet Submits Clerics Council Bill to Parliament",
    "original_title": "Golaha Shacabka oo loo gudbiyey Hindise-Sharciyeedka Dhismaha Golaha Culimada Soomaaliyeed",
    "slug": "somalia-s-cabinet-submits-clerics-council-bill-to-parliament",
    "summary": "The Federal Ministry of Endowment and Islamic Affairs has sent a draft law to the House of the People for establishing the Somali Clerics Council, aiming to unify religious scholars and enhance their role in society.",
    "content": "Mogadishu – On August 24, 2026, the Federal Ministry of Endowment and Islamic Affairs submitted a draft law to the House of the People of the Federal Parliament of Somalia, proposing the establishment of the Somali Clerics Council. The bill now enters the formal legislative approval process, where it will be reviewed and debated before any potential enactment.\n\nMinister of Endowment and Islamic Affairs, Sheikh Mukhtaar Roobow Cali, addressed the House of the People, presenting the key objectives of the draft legislation. He emphasized that the proposed council is intended to unify Somali religious scholars, strengthen their engagement with communities, and foster a structured channel for consultation between the government and the clergy. The minister’s remarks highlighted the government’s view of the council as a mechanism to institutionalize religious guidance within the national framework.\n\nThe move comes amid broader efforts by the federal government to formalize religious institutions and align them with national governance structures. Previous initiatives have sought to regulate religious affairs, but this is a significant legislative step, as it codifies the role of clerics in Somali society. The specific provisions of the bill, including the council’s composition, powers, and responsibilities, have not been fully disclosed, but the ministry has indicated that the council will serve as a coordinating body for religious matters.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is particularly relevant, as the diaspora often turns to religious leaders for guidance on cultural and religious matters, especially in the absence of strong state institutions. A formalized Clerics Council could provide a more unified voice for religious scholars, potentially influencing religious discourse both within Somalia and in diaspora communities. It may also facilitate clearer communication between the government and religious authorities, which could impact social policies and community initiatives.\n\nThe bill’s progression through parliament will be closely watched by observers, as it touches on sensitive issues of religion and governance. The outcome could set a precedent for how the federal government interacts with religious institutions in the future, shaping the landscape of Islamic affairs in Somalia for years to come.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/24/golaha-shacabka-oo-loo-gudbiyey-hindise-sharciyeedka-dhismaha-golaha-culimada-soomaaliyeed/",
