    "id": "news-1787585407031-2",
    "title": "Somalia Assumes Full Responsibility for Justice System as UNTMIS Mandate Ends",
    "original_title": "Soomaaliya oo La Wareegaysa Mas’uuliyadda Horumarinta Nidaamka Cadaaladda Kadib Soo Afjaridda Hawlihii UNTMIS",
    "slug": "somalia-assumes-full-responsibility-for-justice-system-as-untmis-mandate-ends",
    "summary": "Somalia's Ministry of Justice and Constitutional Affairs has taken over full responsibility for developing the justice and prison services following the conclusion of the UN mission's support role, marking a significant shift in the country's governance.",
    "content": "Mogadishu — In a formal transition ceremony held on August 24, 2026, Somalia's Minister of Justice and Constitutional Affairs, Hassan Moalim Mohamud, announced that the federal government has assumed full responsibility for the development of the country's justice and prison systems. The event, which brought together officials from the federal government, regional administrations, representatives from the United Nations and the UN Assistance Mission in Somalia (UNTMIS), as well as international partners, signified the official closure of UNTMIS's role in supporting Somalia's judicial sector. The minister extended gratitude to the UN and its agencies for their years of support, acknowledging that the step is a pivotal moment in Somalia's path toward self-governance and institutional consolidation.\n\nThe transition is part of a broader plan initiated by the UN Security Council to gradually hand over security and development responsibilities to Somali authorities, allowing the mission to phase out its operations by October 2026. Over the past decade, UNTMIS and its predecessor were instrumental in training judges, improving prison conditions, and drafting legal frameworks that align with international standards. Minister Mohamud emphasized that while challenges remain—including limited resources, a backlog of cases, and the need for infrastructure refurbishment—the government is committed to building a fair and accessible justice system for all Somalis.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the move holds particular significance as many look to Somalia for investment, legal recourse, and reconnection with their homeland. A locally driven justice system is expected to bolster the rule of law, protect property rights, and attract diaspora business, which has often been hampered by legal ambiguities. Moreover, the handover signifies a new era of accountability, as Somali institutions will now be directly responsible for upholding human rights standards within judicial and correctional facilities. The ceremony concluded with reaffirmations from international partners that they will continue to provide technical assistance, yet the leadership remains firmly in Somali hands.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/24/soomaaliya-oo-la-wareegaysa-masuuliyadda-horumarinta-nidaamka-cadaaladda-kadib-soo-afjaridda-hawlihii-untmis/",
    "source": "goobjoog.com",
    "source_name": "Goobjoog",
    "original_url": "https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/24/soomaaliya-oo-la-wareegaysa-masuuliyadda-horumarinta-nidaamka-cadaaladda-kadib-soo-afjaridda-hawlihii-untmis/",
    "country": "Somalia",
